  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nAs surface high pressure drifts through the southeastern states\nthrough Tuesday, with its northern extent impacting the Ohio Valley,\ndry weather is expected to continue. A weak disturbance will move\nthrough the Great Lakes on Tuesday morning, but any light \nprecipitation is likely to dissipate before reaching the ILN forecast\narea.\n\nA more active weather pattern is expected to develop by Wednesday. A\nsurface front will set up west-to-east across the northern Ohio\nValley / southern Great Lakes, with the ILN CWA initially in the warm\nsector on Wednesday. The flow of moisture into the region will\nincrease, around a mid-level ridge over the southeastern states.\nChances for rain and storms will begin to increase on Wednesday, with\nsome diurnal component to coverage likely, and the greatest chances\noccurring Wednesday afternoon into the evening. Model soundings are\nfairly moist, but with weak flow and a lack of confidence across the\nmodel suite in instability, the severe threat is uncertain as of now.\nSome heavy rain could occur given the moist environment and potential\nfor a few rounds of storms as the frontal zone slowly moves\nsouthward.\n\nSome additional rain (and possibly convection) will be possible\nThursday into Thursday night, before a well-defined cold front moves\nthrough the region some time Friday -- likely in the early morning\nhours.\n\nTemperatures will likely peak out on Tuesday, with highs in the mid\nto upper 80s, before gradually falling the next few days. A more \nsignificant drop in temperatures is expected behind the cold front on\nFriday, leading to temperatures below normal over the weekend. Lows\nmay get into the 30s on Saturday morning, and will almost certainly\ndrop well into the 30s on Sunday morning, so some frost potential \nmay exist.\n\nFinally, some gusty winds may be possible Friday behind the cold\nfront -- with a well-mixed boundary layer and a tight pressure\ngradient. Not much confidence yet on headline-criteria winds, but\ncurrent projections suggest some gusts above 30kts will be possible.",
